# Letters Home, Aske's side quest (ticket 1072). Written on a blank page
# from the beat sheet's cards (the story-writing skill). Beat sheet:
# docs/story/chapters/side-aske.md. Continuity: docs/story/ledger.md.
# Voice cards: docs/story/characters/*.md and the beat sheet (Casker,
# Sten).
#
# An optional battle on the Act 1 map (worldmaps/act1.ron,
# `letters_home`): it opens once the Envoy's House is held while Aske is
# in the army, and may be fought any time to the end of the act, so
# nothing below leans on the Ashfields or after: Rue never speaks to Aske.
# Scene ids, as battles/side_letters_home.ron wires them:
#
#   Before the battle (intro_scenes)
#     side_letters_intro                 the cliff top: the boat, the hatch
#
#   In the battle (triggers, each once)
#     side_letters_first_turn            TurnStart(turn: 1, phase: Player)
#     side_letters_boat                  UnitEntersArea(who: Faction(Player), the deck)
#     side_letters_boss_engage           CombatStart(unit: "privateer"): anyone else
#     side_letters_boss_engage_poacher   CombatStart(unit: "privateer", against: Some("poacher"))
#     side_letters_boss_half             HalfHp(unit: "privateer")
#     side_letters_boss_fell             UnitFell(unit: "privateer"): wins; the rest run
#     side_letters_death_lead            UnitFell(unit: "lead"): game over in both modes
#     side_letters_death_<id>            UnitFell(unit: "<id>", mode: Some(Classic))
#     side_letters_retreat_<id>          UnitFell(unit: "<id>", mode: Some(Casual))
#       for <id> = retainer, sergeant, poacher, keeper, heretic, defector;
#       Gil's and Hedda's are written below in "@if" blocks and wait for
#       their slots (1071, 0851): wire them then and drop the blocks.
#
#   After the battle (victory_scenes)
#     side_letters_victory               the shingle: Sten, the bow, what her mother said
#
# Sten speaks Brennish: narration says so once, his lines are what he
# says, and Aske says them again for the rest, or doesn't. Hedda is the
# only other one who understands him, so the scene has an "@else" for
# when she isn't there. The validator doesn't know a side quest's scenes
# can count on their character, so Aske's lines before and after the
# battle sit in "@if poacher" blocks like everyone else's (ADR-0055).
#
# Places: the quest's own map, behind the talk. Music: the mood cues are
# this ticket's picks; the battle's cue is in its file.
#
# Tone: the cliff and the battle are played straight; the shingle is a
# quiet scene after, where the warmth is an old man saying a mother's
# words exactly, and a girl translating them wrong.

# The cliff top, evening.

@scene side_letters_intro
@caption {N:place.gull_creek}, the cliff top
@place side_letters_home 10,7
@music talk_calm
> Evening, and wind off the sea. The creek below: a boat beached on the shingle with its mast down, a fire, a sloop at the creek mouth. {n:poacher} is on her belly in the grass.
@if poacher
@left poacher neutral
poacher: That is the hold's boat. The patch on the sail, I know the patch. ...I have counted them. There are people in it. In the hold. They nail the hatch shut, privateers, so the cargo does not walk.
@left clear
@endif
@if keeper
@right keeper sad
keeper: Did the envoy's letter say how many? It said the creek, I know it said the creek, I only-- it's a long way down, isn't it. I'm only saying it's a long way.
@right clear
@endif
@if poacher
@left poacher neutral
poacher: It said where. ...The nailing I know from a hill letter, off a boat they took last spring. {n:keeper} read it to me, I could not get the words.
@left clear
@endif
@if sergeant
@right sergeant neutral
sergeant: Mare's not going down that. I'll bring her round the long way and come in last, don't wait on me. ...Who's slow? Keeper's slow. Keeper goes in the middle.
@right clear
@endif
@if retainer
@right retainer neutral
retainer: Nobody runs for the boat. The fire first. {n:family.veyne} in the middle, same as always.
@right clear
@endif
@if heretic
@right heretic neutral
heretic: Does anybody think I'd burn a boat with people in it? ...I'm saying I know. I'm not going to.
@right clear
@endif
@if shieldbearer
@right shieldbearer neutral
shieldbearer: There'll be a paper on that sloop with the council's wax on it, saying this is lawful. ...I washed a floor for that council three years.
@right clear
@endif
@left lead neutral
lead: Down. Quietly.
@if poacher
@right poacher angry
poacher: I go first. I know the path.
> She is over the edge before anyone can say no.
@right clear
@endif
@end

# In the battle.

@scene side_letters_first_turn
@if poacher
@left poacher neutral
poacher: I have counted them. Count them again, so I am not the only one counting.
@left clear
@else
@if retainer
@left retainer neutral
retainer: The fire first. Nobody runs for the boat.
@left clear
@endif
@endif
@end

@scene side_letters_boat
@if poacher
@left poacher angry
poacher: The hatch. They nailed it, I said they-- I have a knife. I have a knife, get back, I have a knife.
@left clear
@else
> The hatch is nailed shut from above, and under it something moves.
@endif
@end

@scene side_letters_boss_engage
@right privateer neutral
privateer: Letter of marque, council of {n:faction.league}, wax and all. You're the unlawful ones here, friend, and I'll be paid for you too. It's all written down.
@end

@scene side_letters_boss_engage_poacher
@left poacher angry
@right privateer neutral
privateer: The archer. The hill girl. Your silver's in my sea chest with a tag on it, girl, in my own hand. I'm tidy.
poacher: My mor's name is on that tag. You wrote it. You wrote her name.
privateer: I write everything down. The council likes a list.
poacher: Then write mine.
@end

@scene side_letters_boss_half
@right privateer angry
privateer: Back to the sloop, the lot of you, leave the chest-- no! Bring the chest! Bring the chest, you--
@end

@scene side_letters_boss_fell
@right privateer sad
privateer: The paper's in my coat. ...Show them the paper. It was lawful. Somebody tell the council it was lawful.
@end

@scene side_letters_death_lead
@left lead sad
> {lead|The exile} is down on the shingle, and the hatch is still nailed.
@end

@scene side_letters_death_retainer
@left retainer sad
retainer: The hatch. Somebody get the hatch. Not me, somebody-- the old man's got people to get home.
@end

@scene side_letters_retreat_retainer
@left retainer angry
retainer: Knee's gone. Put me against the rock and get on. Get on!
@end

@scene side_letters_death_sergeant
@left sergeant sad
sergeant: The mare's on the cliff top. Somebody go up for her, she'll stand there all night, she-- she'll stand there all night.
@end

@scene side_letters_retreat_sergeant
@left sergeant angry
sergeant: Right. Right, I'm out of it, I'm sitting on this rock-- don't look at me, look at the deck!
@end

@scene side_letters_death_poacher
@left poacher sad
poacher: My letters. The ones with the bad writing. ...Send them anyway. She will not mind the writing.
@end

@scene side_letters_retreat_poacher
@left poacher angry
poacher: I am not done. ...Yes. Yes, I am. Pull me back.
@end

@scene side_letters_death_keeper
@left keeper sad
keeper: The long letter at the end of "come". I was going to show her again, you bring the tail under-- somebody show her. It's only the tail.
@end

@scene side_letters_retreat_keeper
@left keeper sad
keeper: I'm sorry, I'm-- it's the stones, I can't stand on the stones, I'll just-- I'm sitting down. I'm sorry.
@end

@scene side_letters_death_heretic
@left heretic sad
heretic: Did they get the hatch open? ...Get it open. Then feed them. There's a pot on that fire.
@end

@scene side_letters_retreat_heretic
@left heretic angry
heretic: Shingle. Who fights on shingle? Fine, I'm down, I'm-- fine.
@end

@scene side_letters_death_defector
@left defector sad
defector: The sail wants a patch. A proper one, I'd have-- somebody tell them I can't now. That was the right thing to say, wasn't it?
@end

@scene side_letters_retreat_defector
@left defector surprised
defector: I slipped, that's all, the stones-- I'm all right! I'm going back up, I'm going!
@end

# Gil's and Hedda's lines, for when they have slots here (1071, 0851).

@scene side_letters_death_prizefighter
@if prizefighter
@left prizefighter sad
prizefighter: Well struck. ...Champion, the sea's at your back. Mind it.
@left clear
@endif
@end

@scene side_letters_retreat_prizefighter
@if prizefighter
@left prizefighter neutral
prizefighter: I'm sitting. On the shingle. Somebody stand where I was.
@left clear
@endif
@end

@scene side_letters_death_shieldbearer
@if shieldbearer
@left shieldbearer sad
shieldbearer: Hill girl. ...Tell her I heard what the old man said. Every word. Tell her that.
@left clear
@endif
@end

@scene side_letters_retreat_shieldbearer
@if shieldbearer
@left shieldbearer neutral
shieldbearer: Easy. I'm down, that's all. Somebody take the plank.
@left clear
@endif
@end

# The shingle, with the dark coming on.

@scene side_letters_victory
@caption {N:place.gull_creek}, the shingle
@place side_letters_home 13,7
@music scene_sad
> The hatch is up, and hill people come into the air one at a time, coughing. The chest stands open on the stones. Last out is an old man with a bundle in oilcloth, who has to be helped over the side.
> He speaks in {n:faction.brennmark.adj}, and {n:poacher} says it again for the rest. Or doesn't.
@right boatmaster neutral
boatmaster: Your mor's girl. ...You're thinner. Come here where I can see you. No? Then stand there. I'll look from here.
@if poacher
@left poacher neutral
poacher: He says I am thinner.
@left clear
@endif
boatmaster: Your mor sent this south. She said, if the girl will not come home, the girl will have her brother's bow and not get herself killed with some bad Ardevali thing.
boatmaster: She said stop sending it. The hold eats. She said she would rather have the girl than the silver, and I was to say that to your face. So I'm saying it.
@if poacher
@left poacher neutral
poacher: ...He says the hold is well. He says thank you for the boat.
@left clear
@endif
@if shieldbearer
@left shieldbearer neutral
shieldbearer: That is not what he said, hill girl. ...Easy. Say it or don't. But don't say he said that.
@left clear
@else
> He has a few words of Ardevali, and finds one of them.
boatmaster: Her mother. ...Her mother said.
@endif
@if poacher
@left poacher angry
poacher: I know what he said! I was there, I heard him, I do not need-- ...I know what she said.
@right clear
@right lead neutral
@choice
* earnest: Send the silver anyway. And a line in your own hand.
  poacher: I was going to. I did not need you to say it. ...I was going to.
* blunt: Your mother's right.
  poacher: She is not right. She is my mor. It is different. ...Do not say that again. Say it after.
* act: Let me see the bow.
  > {lead|The exile} holds out a hand. She doesn't give it.
  poacher: It is not yours to see. ...Here. Do not pull it. You will not manage it. Nobody here will.
@endchoice
@right clear
@right boatmaster neutral
@left clear
@endif
@if keeper
@left keeper neutral
keeper: The long letter at the end of "come". It's only the tail, you bring it under-- you've written it four times, {n:poacher}. That's four comes. ...Five is fine. Five is all right.
@left clear
@endif
@if poacher
@left poacher neutral
poacher: It is not enough comes. ...Show me the tail again.
@left clear
@endif
@if retainer
@left retainer neutral
retainer: Tide. Get the old man his tide, he's got people to get home. {n:family.veyne}, the plank.
@left clear
@endif
boatmaster: I'll read it to her by the kitchen fire. The bad letters and all. ...I'll say you were thinner. She'll want to know.
@right clear
> The boat comes off the shingle on the tide with its silver and its letters, and one line in bad writing.
@if heretic
> At the far fire, {n:heretic} is ladling the privateers' pot into hill people's hands, and does not look this way once.
@endif
@if poacher
> {n:poacher} stands with the bow in her hand, unstrung, and doesn't watch the boat go.
@endif
@end
